edb/ref 17592186360460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f4bd8552-cbfe-5cdf-9c63-a1215aabe6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449365 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449366 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449367 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449368 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={{t}^{2}+at+b\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- **II.** $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- **IV.** $B$ is a **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4e607a0-6afc-5179-8279-85636fe2408c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125153 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)={t}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "027d54ca-a5e1-5f8e-b0e8-aea8a3580062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125154 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1061 653353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9024def-0495-5d67-911a-9b23824e8187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1061 653361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3832943-8321-5094-b97b-66a6951219cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1061 653358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78c3b58c-ad85-58e8-8baf-c63bf9e544b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1061 653355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f647c92d-413b-597e-a1c7-89a867776426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1061 653354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa129907-91b4-5bfa-8c65-22954ea25d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1065 3205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4274a05d-d402-5dfc-a339-e5a6b5a8e5b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now look at an example of a subset of a vector space that is not a subspace.\n\nConsider the vector space $\\mathbb R_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ at+b \\: | \\: a,b  \\in\\mathbb{Z} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition. Indeed, suppose that $f(t)=a_1t+b_1$ and $g(t)=a_2t+b_2$ are two polynomials from $B.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since $a_1+a_2$ and $b_1+b_2$ are in $\\Bbb Z.$\n\n- $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication. As a counterexample, consider $f(t)=t$ and $k=0.5,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =0.5t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial from $B,$ because $0.5 \\notin \\Bbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is not a subspace of $\\mathbb R_2[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1065 6193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "532d5411-c233-58a0-88e3-bd13bc8fcd1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1067 "Example of a Subset That Is Not a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448967 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448968 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448969 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448970 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89286e95-724b-57e9-aee8-7d71e484cafb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244022 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $A,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an element of $A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ka \\\\ kb & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448989 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448990 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448991 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186448992 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $W$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3ebe5cf-3b69-5829-b6a6-d075f8831709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244034 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452524 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452525 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452526 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452527 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)=f(-1)}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- **IV.** $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ed6c488-d6f4-5c35-b1ec-2f3c2d68e465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329727 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =f(-1)+g(-1) \\\\  & =(f+g)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(1) & =kf(1) \\\\  & =kf(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452816 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452817 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452818 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452819 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{2}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=2\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- **II.** $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **III.** $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\n- **IV.** $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef92c245-5042-5dba-9942-02e46fcd2132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =2+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=2$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2\\cdot 2=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452824 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452825 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452826 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452827 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $E$ containing all diagonal $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $E$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $E$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ffc0d2e-16d3-51e9-8958-0f5807298d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360461 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $E,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $E,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452836 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452837 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452838 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452839 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- **II.** $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **III.** $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\n- **IV.** $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10fb8ec2-9c2e-55ae-85b0-700b4fea54e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $H$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452848 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452850 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452851 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f62d508-5cf6-5993-b139-949d11db546e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360467 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454363 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454364 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454365 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454366 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all nonconstant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $K$ is closed under function addition\n- **II.** $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- **IV.** $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{constant}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecd1a414-f091-5cfd-86f0-d0c82f6d9564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378362 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, the functions $f(x)=x+2$ and $f(x)=-x-1$ are in $K$, however\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=1\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1]$ and hence $f+g$ is a constant function. \nSo, $K$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, the product of the scalar zero by any function is the constant function zero. So, $K$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $K$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454823 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454824 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454825 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454826 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $D={\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a<0}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02488e4e-8845-5c5e-a156-a562e8b67a01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380291 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example,  if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af81d6e6-3cad-5c8a-99a9-13bc107158af" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4921fc50-ffd5-5037-85e5-4437f4da71a5" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7871ce40-048e-5347-b7c4-782324459d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447857 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "54e54e39-08f5-53d6-